<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jam Nizam al-Din II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 1508</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Jam Nindo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jam Nizam al-Din II, known as Jam Nindo, was the most celebrated ruler of the Samma dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a>, reigning from about 1461 to 1508 from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thatta">Thatta</a>. He is remembered as a just and pious ruler who defended Sindh against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arghun">Arghuns</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandahar">Kandahar</a>. His reign was a high point of Samma rule, and his tomb at the Makli necropolis is among its finest monuments.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jam-nizam-al-din-ii.jpg" alt="Jam Nizam al-Din II" width="320" height="301" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Syed HussainHyder Zaidi · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tomb_of_Sultan_Jam_Nizamuddin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
